`fun f(pb: ProcessBuilder) { pb.start() }` needs no import (JLS 7.3), but
kotlin's _kt_receiver_module and scala's typed-receiver call arm accepted
only an import or an inline dotted path for a DECLARED type, so the call
went out as external:0-0:start and classified as nothing. Measured before
the fix through verify-claims on dev 02507e31eb: "No subprocess launches"
came back confirmed_with_caveats (exit 3), the caveat saying the receiver's
type "could not be determined", while the same code spelled
java.lang.ProcessBuilder came back violated (exit 1). Scala disagreed with
itself: its parameterless arm (_library_owner) already took the closed list.

Both now fall through to static_owner_module, the closed java.lang list the
static-call arm (WI-kilap) uses, with the same exclusions: a project type,
an import of another type of the same name, and the names kotlin.* /
scala._ shadow keep the placeholder. Kotlin's docstring recorded the
premise for declining ("a declared type the file forgot to import is not
java.lang's"); that holds for InputStream and is false for ProcessBuilder,
which is why the list is closed. Scala's two branches now share one lookup.

Tests (test_jvm_declared_java_lang_receiver.py, kotlin + scala): the bare
ProcessBuilder / Runtime receivers name java.lang and classify as
subprocess; verify-claims reports the claim violated end to end; a project
ProcessBuilder, an imported org.other.ProcessBuilder and a shadowed name
keep their own answer. 8 of 14 were red before the change.

